1
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:10,960
This is part two of a three-part module discussing approach to interpretation of abdominal and pelvic

2
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:18,240
CTs. Following completion of all three modules, you should be able to employ a systematic search

3
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:26,080
pattern for interpreting abdominal and pelvic CTs. This module specifically will address the

4
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:32,360
search pattern for the bowel, mesentery, and peritoneum. You should be able to identify

5
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:38,520
alterations in the normal anatomy of the bowel and mesentery, as well as be able to identify

6
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:47,000
pneumoperitoneum and ascites. Just as a reminder, what we discuss in this module, specifically the

7
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:55,280
bowel, mesentery, and peritoneum, is just one component of a larger, more comprehensive search

8
00:00:55,280 --> 00:01:02,340
pattern that you will need to employ when evaluating abdominal and pelvic CTs. Take a minute to

9
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:09,780
review this sample dictation, which describes normal findings as they relate to the bowel,

10
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:16,930
mesentery, and peritoneum. When assessing the bowel, you need to be comprehensive.

11
00:01:18,110 --> 00:01:22,890
You want to begin with the evaluation of the distal esophagus, gastroesophageal junction,

12
00:01:23,610 --> 00:01:29,330
stomach and duodenum. You have to evaluate the mesenteric small bowel, that is the jejunum and

13
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:37,350
ileum. And then finally, the large bowel, which you can trace retrograde from the rectum to include

14
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:46,330
the appendix. The primary features to assess when evaluating the bowel on CT imaging include the

15
00:01:46,330 --> 00:01:52,370
caliber of the bowel and the bowel wall. You want to evaluate the caliber to assure that it's not

16
00:01:52,370 --> 00:01:57,810
dilated or narrowed. And you want to assess the bowel wall for overall thickness of the wall,

17
00:01:57,810 --> 00:02:06,790
as well as abnormalities in the attenuation or enhancement of the wall itself. In terms of bowel

18
00:02:06,790 --> 00:02:12,030
wall thickening, if it's present, you need to then take the next step and try to characterize

19
00:02:12,030 --> 00:02:19,530
the thickening. First of all, in terms of its distribution, is it eccentric within the bowel,

20
00:02:19,530 --> 00:02:27,070
or does it involve the entire circumference of the bowel? Is it focal, like a mass, or is it over a

21
00:02:27,070 --> 00:02:34,190
short segment, or is it diffused? Does it involve the whole colon or their entire small bowel? You

22
00:02:34,190 --> 00:02:39,970
want to assess what part of the bowel is affected. Is it the small bowel, the colon, or both? And then

23
00:02:39,970 --> 00:02:46,630
finally, within each type of bowel, is it involved primarily the proximal portion of the bowel

24
00:02:46,630 --> 00:02:54,090
or distal portion of the bowel? In addition to characterizing the distribution of the bowel

25
00:02:54,090 --> 00:02:59,910
wall thickening, you want to evaluate the wall attenuation. That is, does the bowel wall contain

26
00:02:59,910 --> 00:03:06,450
gas? So that would be pneumatosis. Is there high attenuation within the bowel wall itself, which is

27
00:03:06,450 --> 00:03:11,750
often indicative of hemorrhage? If intravenous contrast was administered, you want to assess

28
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:17,510
the pattern of enhancement. Maybe there's a lack of an enhancement, which would be indicative of ischemia.

29
00:03:17,510 --> 00:03:23,610
Or maybe there's a certain pattern of bowel wall enhancement that may give you a clue to an

30
00:03:23,610 --> 00:03:29,810
etiology. You also want to assess the adjacent mesentery. It's useful to think of the bowel and

31
00:03:29,810 --> 00:03:36,890
mesentery as a single unit. And then finally, consider the clinical setting, which may be a

32
00:03:36,890 --> 00:03:40,390
clue to the etiology of the bowel wall abnormality.

33
00:03:40,390 --> 00:03:48,670
Here are some examples in terms of distribution of bowel wall thickening. The image on the left shows

34
00:03:48,670 --> 00:03:57,210
focal wall thickening involving the colon that turned out to be a colon cancer. The image in the middle

35
00:03:57,210 --> 00:04:05,730
shows segmental wall thickening involving the sigmoid colon from colonic diverticulitis. And notice how this

36
00:04:05,730 --> 00:04:14,110
affects a short segment of the bowel. The image on the right shows diffuse small bowel wall thickening.

37
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:21,340
Here are some more examples of the features that you need to assess in terms of distribution of bowel

38
00:04:21,340 --> 00:04:27,940
wall thickening. The image on the left is the same image that we discussed on the previous slide in terms

39
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:33,440
of the focal abnormality. But here I'm also pointing out that the bowel wall thickening is shown here is

40
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:40,860
eccentric. That is, it involves only one portion of the bowel wall or one side. As you can see, the other

41
00:04:40,860 --> 00:04:48,180
side is not affected. Again, this is a colonic adenocarcinoma causing focal and eccentric bowel

42
00:04:48,180 --> 00:04:55,960
wall thickening. The image on the right shows a thickened bowel segment, but notice that both sides of

43
00:04:55,960 --> 00:05:01,980
the wall are affected. And when you look at this in all of the planes, you realize that it's actually

44
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:10,440
circumferential. So this involves the entire circumference of the bowel. One thing that you do need to be aware

45
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:17,860
of is that under distended bowel can appear thickened when in fact it's normal. So this is a pitfall when

46
00:05:17,860 --> 00:05:24,820
assessing for bowel wall thickening. So if you look at this image in the non-dependent or anterior

47
00:05:24,820 --> 00:05:32,900
portions of the abdomen, we have some distended bowel loops. However, more posteriorly or along the

48
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:39,100
dorsal aspect of the peritoneal cavity, we have some under distended bowel segments. And if you're not

49
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:44,260
careful, you may think that this represents true bowel wall thickening, when in fact it's an artifact

50
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:50,620
from luminal under distension. In addition to evaluating the thickness of the wall, you need to

51
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:58,340
evaluate the attenuation or enhancement of the wall. Notice that the image on the left shows

52
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:04,740
circumferential thickening of the small bowel. Contrast was administered, so we need to assess

53
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:13,340
the enhancement of the bowel wall. Here the enhancement is uniform. That's in contrast to the image on the

54
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:20,100
right, which shows a circumferentially thickened small bowel loop in the right lower quadrant. But

55
00:06:20,100 --> 00:06:27,500
notice the appearance of the bowel here. There's sort of different layers of the bowel wall that you can

56
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:32,860
identify because some are enhancing more than the other. So sort of the mucosal surface and the outer

57
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:40,780
surface are enhancing more than that inner ring. And that gives a targetoid appearance of the bowel. And

58
00:06:40,780 --> 00:06:46,780
this is often termed mural stratification. So these are different patterns of bowel wall enhancement.

59
00:06:48,060 --> 00:06:54,460
This is another example of abnormal bowel wall attenuation. In this case, there is gas within the

60
00:06:54,460 --> 00:07:00,860
bowel wall and that's termed pneumatosis. This can often be more easily identified on a lung or bone

61
00:07:00,860 --> 00:07:08,380
window as compared to your standard soft tissue window as shown in this example. Here we can see

62
00:07:10,060 --> 00:07:18,700
gas within the wall of the small bowel which is more easily identified on the lung or bone window.

63
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:29,060
When evaluating caliber of the small and large bowel, you want to be able to identify bowel dilatation.

64
00:07:29,620 --> 00:07:35,220
This may mean obstruction. So if you have an obstruction of the bowel, you will tend to get

65
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:43,780
dilatation of the bowel upstream relative to the site of obstruction. However, it is important to

66
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:50,580
remember that not all bowel dilatation is secondary to obstruction. And there are non-obstructive

67
00:07:50,580 --> 00:07:58,500
etiologies that include an adynamic ileus, ischemia, or pseudo-obstruction, particularly in the colon.

68
00:08:00,570 --> 00:08:06,410
One of the things that you will commonly be asked to evaluate for on an abdominal and pelvic CT is the

69
00:08:06,410 --> 00:08:15,050
presence or absence of small bowel obstruction. When you are evaluating a patient for bowel obstruction,

70
00:08:15,050 --> 00:08:21,130
you have several goals. So first of all, you want to identify, okay, is bowel obstruction present or not?

71
00:08:21,930 --> 00:08:28,650
If small bowel obstruction is present, you want to identify the location of the obstruction

72
00:08:28,650 --> 00:08:35,610
obstruction and specifically the transition point. That is where the bowel exactly is obstructed.

73
00:08:36,490 --> 00:08:42,010
You also need to identify the etiology of this obstruction. And you can remember back to learning

74
00:08:42,010 --> 00:08:49,370
the ABCs of small bowel obstruction in terms of common etiologies, adhesions, bulges, which really means

75
00:08:49,370 --> 00:08:56,330
hernias and cancer. And then finally, you need to assess for possible complications of that obstruction,

76
00:08:56,330 --> 00:09:04,970
including bowel ischemia and perforation. Let's look at some examples of imaging findings that are

77
00:09:04,970 --> 00:09:13,450
present with small bowel obstruction. The image on the left shows a double-headed arrow indicating

78
00:09:13,450 --> 00:09:22,890
dilated small bowel. The image on the right looks at air fluid levels within a given small bowel

79
00:09:22,890 --> 00:09:32,090
loop. And notice that the air fluid levels are actually at different levels in the same

80
00:09:32,090 --> 00:09:38,170
loop of bowel. And that differs in the gravity-dependent direction. So the patient for CT is supine.

81
00:09:38,170 --> 00:09:45,610
And so in the anterior-posterior plane, notice that those air fluid levels are actually at different

82
00:09:45,610 --> 00:09:51,850
positions. And that's indicative of the bowel still being able to peristals against a fixed obstruction.

83
00:09:51,850 --> 00:09:57,450
And you happen to be imaging when those air fluid levels are sort of moving around. And again,

84
00:09:57,450 --> 00:10:04,410
that has to be in the same loop of bowel. You have to be comparing gas fluid levels in the same bowel loop.

85
00:10:05,130 --> 00:10:08,970
And in the gravity-dependent direction, when they're at different levels like that,

86
00:10:09,690 --> 00:10:15,930
that is termed a dynamic air fluid level. If they are at the same level, that would be considered

87
00:10:15,930 --> 00:10:21,290
a static air fluid level. Now, static air fluid levels can be present in both an ileus or an

88
00:10:21,290 --> 00:10:27,210
obstruction. But if you see a dynamic air fluid level, then that should make you think more of an

89
00:10:27,210 --> 00:10:32,730
obstruction rather than an ileus. Because with an ileus, the fundamental problem is the bowel is not

90
00:10:32,730 --> 00:10:39,290
peristalsing. And so that fluid within the bowel just tends to settle in the gravity-dependent direction

91
00:10:39,850 --> 00:10:48,140
at the same level. This is an example of the transition point of a small bowel obstruction.

92
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:54,140
We have three successive images from cranial to cotta as we go from top left to bottom.

93
00:10:55,260 --> 00:11:03,980
The yellow areas show dilated small bowel. If we look on the bottom image anteriorly and to the right,

94
00:11:03,980 --> 00:11:11,100
the blue arrow indicates small bowel. And notice that this small bowel is completely decompressed.

95
00:11:11,660 --> 00:11:17,500
So we have dilated proximal small bowel and decompressed distal small bowel. Somewhere in

96
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:24,220
between there is likely to be the actual site of transition. And we can identify that as shown

97
00:11:24,220 --> 00:11:31,180
by the white arrow. And if you were to follow the bowel in a stack of images, you would see that

98
00:11:31,180 --> 00:11:37,740
the white arrow represents the transition point where you go from dilated bowel to decompressed

99
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:43,740
bowel. So one of your goals when you identify a potential bowel obstruction is to actually

100
00:11:43,740 --> 00:11:52,940
to try to find the exact transition point. Small bowel obstruction complications include

101
00:11:52,940 --> 00:12:00,460
ischemia and perforation. Bowel ischemia can manifest itself as an abnormality in the bowel wall itself,

102
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:07,660
either in terms of thickening or thinning or abnormal attenuation in terms of its enhancement

103
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:16,380
or gas within the bowel wall. So pneumatosis. The example shown here shows pneumatosis within the

104
00:12:17,820 --> 00:12:27,420
small bowel. In addition, this patient also has gas which then progressed draining from that abnormal bowel

105
00:12:27,420 --> 00:12:34,620
bowel into the mesenteric vessels and ultimately into the portal vein. So there was portal venous gas

106
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:41,100
present. The image on the right shows pneumoperitoneum or free intraperitoneal gas as a consequence

107
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:51,080
of bowel perforation. Diverticular or outpouchings of the bowel. They are most commonly identified in the

108
00:12:51,080 --> 00:12:57,800
colon and the duodenum. The image on the left shows a duodenal diverticulum at its characteristic

109
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:08,040
location along the mesial margin of the second portion of the duodenum, often in the region of the pancreatic

110
00:13:08,040 --> 00:13:16,600
head. And then finally the image on the right shows multiple diverticula within the colon with the sigmoid

111
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:25,560
colon typically being the most affected colonic segment. So as you assess the bowel, again you want to start at

112
00:13:25,560 --> 00:13:31,960
the distal esophagus, evaluate the GE junction, stomach and duodenum. And you can look at the mesenteric

113
00:13:31,960 --> 00:13:39,960
small bowel, that is the jejunum and ileum. Then you want to evaluate the colon. Whereas the small bowel

114
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:47,240
is too long and convoluted to trace out in its entirety, you can do so with the colon. I typically

115
00:13:47,240 --> 00:13:53,560
start at the level of the rectum and then trace the colon retrograde looking for bowel wall thickening,

116
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:58,840
caliber changes, etc. like we have talked about. And you can follow that in a retrograde fashion

117
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:04,040
all the way back to the cecum. And eventually you should try to be able to find the appendix if it's

118
00:14:04,040 --> 00:14:10,280
present in every patient. So one of the things you want to evaluate for first and foremost is the

119
00:14:10,280 --> 00:14:17,080
appendix present or not. If it is present, what's the caliber, wall thickness and are there any

120
00:14:17,080 --> 00:14:24,920
inflammatory changes in the adjacent mesentery. Here's an example of appendicitis which will be

121
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:35,560
the most common abnormality of the appendix that you will identify. When tracing the colon in a retrograde

122
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:41,480
fashion from the rectum to the cecum, once you identify the ileocecal valve and are moving in that

123
00:14:41,480 --> 00:14:49,720
retrograde way from the ileocecal valve, you know you're within the cecum. Often you can identify the

124
00:14:50,520 --> 00:15:01,080
appendix arising from the cecum a few centimeters from the ileocecal valve. So in this example on the

125
00:15:01,080 --> 00:15:10,520
coronal image on the left we have a contrast filled cecum and notice the finger-like tubular

126
00:15:10,520 --> 00:15:17,480
structure adjacent to that that is blind ending and is not filled with contrast. And that's an

127
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:24,440
inflamed appendix and because of the inflammation at the appendiceal orifice, that orifice is closed

128
00:15:24,440 --> 00:15:32,360
off or obstructed so the oral contrast material can't make its way into the appendiceal lumen.

129
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:41,720
On the axial image we can see a dilated appendix and some subtle inflammatory changes in the adjacent

130
00:15:42,520 --> 00:15:48,280
mesentery. Again, in keeping with that principle that you want to kind of think of bowel findings in

131
00:15:48,280 --> 00:15:56,440
association with mesenteric findings and thinking of the bowel and mesentery as a single unit.

132
00:16:04,280 --> 00:16:06,760
This question tests your ability to characterize

133
00:16:08,360 --> 00:16:14,280
bowel wall thickening in terms of its distribution. In this example, the bowel wall thickening is best

134
00:16:14,280 --> 00:16:22,360
characterized as being focal and eccentric. That is, it eccentrically involves the bowel wall in terms of

135
00:16:23,080 --> 00:16:30,360
circumference. So you have involvement of the ventral wall and not really the dorsal wall.

136
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:34,760
Focal and eccentric bowel wall thickening are suggestive of

137
00:16:35,480 --> 00:16:39,720
neoplasm. And again, this is a case of a colonic adenocarcinoma.

138
00:16:42,040 --> 00:16:50,120
After you assess the bowel, you should then specifically look at the adjacent mesentery and greater

139
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:57,560
momentum. Looking for things like edema or fluid, signs of inflammation or enlarged lymph nodes or masses.

140
00:16:59,720 --> 00:17:06,200
Remember that the mesentery contains fat vessels and lymph nodes. One of the main things that you want

141
00:17:06,200 --> 00:17:14,440
to evaluate for is that the mesentery has its normal fat attenuation. And so oftentimes this will be

142
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:21,960
replaced by soft tissue or fluid in the presence of an abnormality. So for example, the image on the left

143
00:17:21,960 --> 00:17:29,880
shows an increased attenuation of the fat of the root of the mesentery, which is secondary to edema

144
00:17:31,160 --> 00:17:37,800
from adjacent inflammation of the circumferentially thickened small bowel.

145
00:17:37,800 --> 00:17:45,640
The image on the right shows engorgement of the vasorecta, so those small vessels

146
00:17:46,360 --> 00:17:54,840
that supply the bowel wall. This is a sign of inflammation and hyperemia in a patient with inflammatory

147
00:17:55,560 --> 00:18:02,520
bowel disease. So these are both examples of how manifestations of bowel pathology show up

148
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:11,240
in the mesentery, either in the mesenteric vasculature or by replacement of the normal fat attenuation

149
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:22,520
within the mesentery. You can also identify masses within the mesentery, not only enlarged lymph nodes,

150
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:30,200
but other solid masses as well as fluid collections and abscesses. Here are two examples. The image on the

151
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:37,960
left is a carcinoid tumor that represents a metastasis from a small bowel primary in the adjacent small

152
00:18:37,960 --> 00:18:45,640
bowel. The image on the right is an example of mesenteric lymphoma. Now the specific diagnoses aren't

153
00:18:45,640 --> 00:18:53,640
important at this point. You just need to realize that you need to evaluate the mesentery in conjunction

154
00:18:53,640 --> 00:19:00,360
with the bowel, not only for things like inflammation and hyperemia, but also for masses and fluid collections.

155
00:19:01,960 --> 00:19:09,160
Don't forget to assess the greater omentum when you're evaluating the mesentery and peritoneum. Here

156
00:19:09,160 --> 00:19:19,690
we see an example of multiple metastasis within the greater omentum seen on both images here in a patient

157
00:19:19,690 --> 00:19:29,220
with ascites and peritoneal carcinomatosis secondary to ovarian cancer. One principle that I do want you

158
00:19:29,220 --> 00:19:35,140
to keep in mind is that you really should think of the bowel and mesentery as a single unit such that

159
00:19:35,140 --> 00:19:41,780
when you identify an abnormality in one you really have to look carefully at the other. So as an example,

160
00:19:41,780 --> 00:19:49,620
here we have a patient with marked thickening of the stomach wall and adjacent infiltration into the

161
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:58,020
perigastric fat in a patient that had not only gastric carcinoma but one that was actually locally invasive

162
00:19:58,020 --> 00:20:09,540
into the adjacent mesentery. You also need to identify the presence of fluid within the abdomen and pelvis.

163
00:20:09,540 --> 00:20:18,100
Typically this will be free fluid in the peritoneal cavity and that's termed ascites. In a typical supine

164
00:20:18,100 --> 00:20:25,380
patient, you want to evaluate the hepatorenal fossa which is the most dependent portion of the peritoneal cavity

165
00:20:25,380 --> 00:20:34,900
in the abdomen and then in the pelvis you want to identify the pouch of douglas in females or the

166
00:20:35,540 --> 00:20:42,820
recto vesicular pouch in males those being the most dependent portions of the peritoneal cavity

167
00:20:42,820 --> 00:20:50,100
for males and females. Once you identify acidic fluid or free fluid in the peritoneal cavity

168
00:20:50,100 --> 00:20:57,460
you want to measure its attenuation. Is it simple fluid or complex fluids such as hemorrhage or infected

169
00:20:58,420 --> 00:21:05,140
ascites and then you also want to evaluate the status of the peritoneum that is is it thickened or not and if it's

170
00:21:05,140 --> 00:21:11,780
thickened is it diffusely thickened or is it focally thickened or sort of mass-like thickening. You can do

171
00:21:11,780 --> 00:21:19,380
something similar in the retroperitoneum looking for fluid collections characterizing their location in

172
00:21:19,380 --> 00:21:24,580
terms of the retroperitoneal spaces that we've previously described and then characterizing the

173
00:21:24,580 --> 00:21:34,020
fluid as either simple or complex. Once you identify acidic fluid or fluid within the peritoneal cavity you want

174
00:21:34,020 --> 00:21:40,580
to characterize its attenuation to determine if it's simple or complex fluid. The image on the left

175
00:21:40,580 --> 00:21:47,140
is a patient with cirrhosis and large volume ascites as shown here. If you measure the attenuation this is

176
00:21:47,140 --> 00:21:53,940
simple fluid. Remember that simple fluid attenuation will typically be between negative 10 to positive 20

177
00:21:53,940 --> 00:22:02,740
Hounsfield units. Contrast that appearance to the fluid in the pelvic peritoneal spaces on the right-sided image.

178
00:22:02,740 --> 00:22:12,150
Here notice how that fluid appears higher in attenuation. Hemorrhage will classically be between

179
00:22:12,150 --> 00:22:19,190
40 and 70 Hounsfield units although this will vary but anything over 20 Hounsfield units you should at

180
00:22:19,190 --> 00:22:27,910
least begin thinking of the possibility of something like hemorrhage or infected fluid. In addition to

181
00:22:27,910 --> 00:22:34,630
assessing the attenuation of the acidic fluid you also need to evaluate the status of the peritoneal

182
00:22:35,270 --> 00:22:39,910
lining. That is is it normal or is it abnormal in terms of enhancement

183
00:22:40,630 --> 00:22:44,950
or thickening? The image on the left shows acidic fluid

184
00:22:45,510 --> 00:22:51,110
but also note that the peritoneum is markedly abnormal in terms of its

185
00:22:51,670 --> 00:22:57,350
thickness and enhancement. The image on the right shows more acidic fluid with more subtle

186
00:22:58,150 --> 00:23:05,350
enhancement and thickening of the peritoneal lining. In general if you identify peritoneal

187
00:23:05,350 --> 00:23:12,230
thickening that's smooth that's not really specific and may be neoplastic or non-neoplastic once you

188
00:23:12,230 --> 00:23:19,110
start to get nodular areas of thickening or irregular thickening then you need to really think that this is

189
00:23:19,110 --> 00:23:28,230
most likely to be a neoplastic process. In addition to evaluating for free fluid in the peritoneal cavity

190
00:23:28,230 --> 00:23:35,270
or ascites you need to assess for the presence of extraluminal gas. So free gas within the peritoneal

191
00:23:35,270 --> 00:23:41,750
cavity is termed pneumoperitoneum. Here are some examples of pneumoperitoneum. The image on the

192
00:23:41,750 --> 00:23:50,150
right shows acidic fluid but in addition to the extraluminal fluid there is gas that is layering in

193
00:23:50,150 --> 00:23:58,150
the non-dependent portion of the peritoneal cavity in this supine patient that's anteriorly. So you have

194
00:23:58,150 --> 00:24:04,870
sort of a gas fluid level within the peritoneal cavity secondary to the presence of both fluid and gas.

195
00:24:05,990 --> 00:24:13,270
The image on the right shows free intraperitoneal gas. Again this will typically be identified in the

196
00:24:13,270 --> 00:24:21,110
most non-dependent portion of the peritoneal cavity and for CT because the patients are supine this will

197
00:24:21,110 --> 00:24:30,470
be in the anterior or ventral of the peritoneal cavity. When trying to detect extraluminal gas

198
00:24:31,830 --> 00:24:38,870
as we previously discussed with nematosis it's often easier to identify this if you use a wider window

199
00:24:38,870 --> 00:24:45,910
either a typical bone or lung window or something in between. In this case illustrates that principle.

200
00:24:47,110 --> 00:24:54,150
There is pneumoperitoneum present on the image on the left which is shown in soft tissue windows although

201
00:24:54,150 --> 00:24:59,110
I think it is very difficult to really appreciate that. However if you look at that in a wider window

202
00:24:59,110 --> 00:25:09,030
I think the gas is easier to identify these triangular collections outside the bowel wall lumen in the

203
00:25:09,030 --> 00:25:18,710
non-dependent or anterior portion of the abdominal cavity. In terms of fluid within the retroperitoneum

204
00:25:20,550 --> 00:25:26,070
the principles are similar. If you identify a fluid collection you want to localize it to one of the

205
00:25:26,070 --> 00:25:32,150
retroperitoneal spaces because that will help you in terms of identifying the potential source. So if you

206
00:25:32,150 --> 00:25:38,550
see something in the anterior perarenal space your first choices for source of that collection would be

207
00:25:38,550 --> 00:25:47,270
structures in the anterior perarenal space such as the pancreas or duodenum. In addition you want to

208
00:25:47,270 --> 00:25:53,190
assess the complexity of the fluid just like we talked about with ascites. So if you see fluid you want to

209
00:25:53,190 --> 00:26:01,670
measure its attenuation to identify if it's simple or complex fluid. This is an example of retroperitoneal

210
00:26:01,670 --> 00:26:09,750
hemorrhage. Notice that it is displacing the kidney anteriorly so the kidney is a retroperitoneal structure

211
00:26:09,750 --> 00:26:14,870
so if it is displaced anteriorly then you know the collection must be in the retroperitoneum.

212
00:26:15,510 --> 00:26:19,190
Also note that the attenuation of the

213
00:26:21,430 --> 00:26:26,630
fluid is higher than that that you would expect for simple fluid and if you measured this on the

214
00:26:26,630 --> 00:26:34,390
PACS workstation that would that would be true. This is an example of retroperitoneal hemorrhage.

215
00:26:41,300 --> 00:26:45,460
This question tests your ability to appropriately diagnose pneumatosis.

216
00:26:45,460 --> 00:26:54,820
Now there's no extraluminal fluid here to suggest ascites or abscess. There is extraluminal gas

217
00:26:55,620 --> 00:27:00,980
but the distribution of the gas as you'll notice in these images which is i think a little bit easier

218
00:27:00,980 --> 00:27:08,180
to identify on the lung window is that it follows the course of the bowel wall. So this is not

219
00:27:08,180 --> 00:27:15,060
in the peritoneal cavity but this is extraluminal gas that's actually in the bowel wall itself

220
00:27:15,940 --> 00:27:24,700
and that is pneumatosis not pneumoperitoneum. So in summary in this module we discussed evaluation of

221
00:27:24,700 --> 00:27:33,340
the bowel mesentery and peritoneum. When assessing the bowel I think it's useful to do it in an anatomical

222
00:27:33,340 --> 00:27:39,420
way that is tracing out the bowel as best you can beginning at the distal esophagus into the

223
00:27:39,420 --> 00:27:45,420
gastroesophageal junction the stomach and then duodenum. Once you get into the mesenteric small

224
00:27:45,420 --> 00:27:51,420
bowel you sort of have to do this in different segments or quadrants looking at the jejunum and

225
00:27:51,420 --> 00:27:57,900
ilium finally down to the ileocecal valve. I then switch gears to the colon and I start

226
00:27:57,900 --> 00:28:05,500
at the rectum and trace the rectum into the sigmoid colon and then in a retrograde fashion

227
00:28:05,500 --> 00:28:13,500
all the way to the level of the ileocecal valve again to identify then the cecum and ultimately the

228
00:28:13,500 --> 00:28:19,820
appendix. While we are evaluating the bowel we are looking for things like bowel caliber that is

229
00:28:19,820 --> 00:28:27,100
is it dilated or narrowed bowel wall thickening and bowel attenuation or enhancement.

230
00:28:28,460 --> 00:28:34,700
In the mesentery we're looking for masses fluid collections or replacement of the normal fat

231
00:28:34,700 --> 00:28:42,220
attenuation by soft tissue or fluid attenuating material. Again the principle to think of is that

232
00:28:42,220 --> 00:28:48,380
the bowel and mesentery should really be kind of thought of as a single unit and evaluated together

233
00:28:48,380 --> 00:28:56,060
so if you find a subtle abnormality in one then you need to look at the other component of that unit.

234
00:28:56,060 --> 00:29:03,580
Assessment of the peritoneal cavity includes evaluation for fluid and gas that is ascites

235
00:29:03,580 --> 00:29:12,150
or pneumoperitoneum. In the peritoneal cavity for fluid evaluation you want to look in the dependent

236
00:29:12,150 --> 00:29:20,540
portions of the peritoneal cavity. When evaluating for pneumoperitoneum you want to evaluate the

237
00:29:20,540 --> 00:29:27,500
non-dependent portions or anteriorly within the abdominal cavity since these patients will be supine.

238
00:29:27,500 --> 00:29:35,900
When evaluating ascites and if ascites is present you need to assess its attenuation to differentiate

239
00:29:35,900 --> 00:29:42,300
simple ascites from complex ascites and you need to assess the status of the peritoneum that is is it

240
00:29:42,300 --> 00:29:49,500
thickened or abnormally enhancing. You can do something similar in the retroperitoneum where if you identify

241
00:29:50,620 --> 00:29:57,740
extraluminal fluid you want to assess its attenuation and then in the retroperitoneum it's important to

242
00:29:57,740 --> 00:30:03,180
localize it to one of the retroperitoneal spaces because that can help you identify

243
00:30:03,180 --> 00:30:13,020
potential sources for that fluid. That brings us to the end of this unit. Please complete the corresponding

244
00:30:13,020 --> 00:30:26,380
quiz questions to receive credit for this course.

